### What is method hiding vs method overriding?

The critical difference is when and how the method call is resolved.

### How do instance methods and static methods behave differently?

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class Account {
    // Instance method: will be overridden
    public String getDescription() { return "Generic Account"; }

    // Static method: will be hidden
    public static String getPolicy() { return "Standard Banking Policy"; }
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    // Overrides parent's instance method
    @Override
    public String getDescription() { return "Savings Account (4% interest)"; }

    // Hides parent's static method (not @Override: hiding)
    public static String getPolicy() { return "Savings Special Policy"; }
}
```

### What happens through a parent reference?

```java
Account ref = new SavingsAccount("SAV001", java.math.BigDecimal.ZERO);

// Instance method: resolved at runtime (actual object = SavingsAccount)
String desc = ref.getDescription();
// Result: "Savings Account (4% interest)" -- JVM dispatches to SavingsAccount

// Static method: resolved at compile time (reference type = Account)
String policy = ref.getPolicy();
// Result: "Standard Banking Policy" -- Account's version, NOT SavingsAccount's
// Compiler sees ref is Account -> binds to Account.getPolicy().
```

### How can you prove it is hiding and not overriding?

Change the reference type without changing the object. The static result changes but the instance result does not.

```java
SavingsAccount saRef = new SavingsAccount("SAV001", java.math.BigDecimal.ZERO);

// Same object, child reference
String saPolicy = saRef.getPolicy();       // "Savings Special Policy"
String saDesc = saRef.getDescription();     // "Savings Account (4% interest)"

// Same object, parent reference
Account accRef = saRef;
String accPolicy = accRef.getPolicy();     // "Standard Banking Policy" (changed!)
String accDesc = accRef.getDescription();   // "Savings Account (4% interest)" (same)

// The object did not change. Only the reference type changed.
// Static result changed -> compile-time binding to reference type.
// Instance result stayed -> runtime binding to actual object type.
```

### Why can static methods not be overridden?

Static methods belong to the class, not to an instance. They are resolved at compile time using the class name. There is no vtable entry for static methods, so no dynamic dispatch mechanism exists.

### What is the practical impact?

If a developer calls a static method on an instance variable expecting polymorphism, the result is wrong.

```java
public class AccountService {
    public void process(Account account) {
        // Instance: works polymorphically
        String desc = account.getDescription(); // correct: SavingsAccount version

        // Static: does NOT work polymorphically
        String policy = account.getPolicy(); // always Account's version!
        // Developer thinks: "I have a SavingsAccount, so getPolicy() returns savings policy."
        // Reality: ref type is Account -> Account.getPolicy() called.
    }
}

// FIX: never call static methods on instance variables.
// Use: Account.getPolicy() or SavingsAccount.getPolicy() explicitly.
```

### How do overriding and hiding compare?

| Aspect | Overriding (instance) | Hiding (static) |
|--------|----------------------|------------------|
| Method type | Instance | Static |
| Resolution time | Runtime | Compile time |
| Based on | Actual object type | Reference variable type |
| Polymorphism | Yes (dynamic dispatch) | No (static binding) |
| @Override | Required | Not applicable |
| Vtable entry | Yes | No |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is calling static methods on instance references and expecting polymorphism.

```java
// WRONG: expecting dynamic dispatch for static method
Account ref = new SavingsAccount();
ref.getPolicy(); // returns Account's policy, not SavingsAccount's

// CORRECT: call static methods on class name
Account.getPolicy();         // "Standard Banking Policy"
SavingsAccount.getPolicy();  // "Savings Special Policy"
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- IntelliJ IDEA warns when static methods are called on instance references because it misleads about the binding mechanism.
- SonarQube rule S2209 flags this pattern as a code smell.
- In bytecode, instance methods use `invokevirtual` (dynamic dispatch), static methods use `invokestatic` (no dispatch).
